## The bug

`ArticleController.create()` is the only handler in the file that doesn't return the RealWorld envelope.

`src/article/article.controller.ts:51-54`

```ts
@Post()
async create(@User('id') userId: number, @Body('article') articleData: CreateArticleDto) {
  return this.articleService.create(userId, articleData);   // ← bare ArticleEntity
}
```

`ArticleService.create()` is typed `Promise<ArticleEntity>` (`article.service.ts:170`) and returns `newArticle` directly (`:186`). So `POST /articles` responds with:

```json
{ "id": 42, "slug": "how-to-train-your-dragon-fj3k2a", "title": "..." }
```

but every other article endpoint — and the RealWorld spec every frontend is written against — responds with `{ "article": { ... } }`. The client does `response.article.slug`, `response.article` is `undefined`, hence **`Cannot read property 'slug' of undefined`**.

**The server logs nothing because nothing failed on the server.** The insert succeeded, Nest returned `201`, the article is really in the database. The contract broke, not the code — that's why it only shows up as a client-side TypeError. Refreshing the article list will show the article you "failed" to create.

The missing return-type annotation on the handler is the tell: every other method is declared `Promise<ArticleRO>` / `Promise<ArticlesRO>`; this one has none, which is what let it drift. `LESSONS.md` records this same class of regression (upstream #57).

## Fix

Two lines, in the service (so the type checker enforces it from now on):

```ts
// article.service.ts
async create(userId: number, articleData: CreateArticleDto): Promise<ArticleRO> {
  ...
  return { article: newArticle };
}

// article.controller.ts
@Post()
async create(@User('id') userId: number, @Body('article') articleData: CreateArticleDto): Promise<ArticleRO> {
  return this.articleService.create(userId, articleData);
}
```

## Two things that will bite you immediately after

While reading `create()` I found the same handler has two more defects — you'll hit them the moment the envelope fix lets the client render the article:

1. **`body` is never saved.** `CreateArticleDto` has `body` (`dto/create-article.dto.ts:4`) and the entity has a `body` column, but `create()` copies `title`, `description`, `slug`, `tagList`, `comments` and skips it (`article.service.ts:172-177`). The column defaults to `''`, so it fails silently — every article is created with an empty body. Add `article.body = articleData.body;`.

2. **`author` is absent from the response.** `newArticle` is captured at `:179`, *before* the author is associated at `:181-184`, so the returned object has no `author` key. A frontend that renders `article.author.username` off the create response crashes the same way. Set `article.author = author` before the save, or re-read the article with `relations: ['author']`.

Want me to apply all three?

